\chapter{Fast status for shell prompts}%
\label{prompt}

A shell prompt that shows the active todos and the currently tracked
labels must run on \emph{every} prompt render.
The obvious implementation runs [[nytid todo status]] and
[[nytid track status --who $USER]], which together take about
1.2~seconds---long enough that pressing return feels sluggish.
Profiling shows that roughly 90~percent of that time is import tax:
importing \emph{anything} under [[nytid.cli]] triggers the
subcommand-discovery loop (\cref{climodule}), which imports every
subcommand group and, transitively, [[typer]], [[rich]],
[[icalendar]], [[requests]] and friends.
The actual work---reading two or three small JSON files---takes
milliseconds.

This chapter provides [[nytid.prompt]], a module that answers exactly
the two questions a prompt asks, without paying the import tax.
The module lives \emph{outside} the [[nytid.cli]] package on purpose:
[[nytid/__init__.py]] is an empty package marker, so
[[import nytid.prompt]] pulls in almost nothing.
The target is at most about 60~milliseconds per invocation, which is
essentially the cost of starting the interpreter and parsing a few
small JSON files.

The price we pay is duplication: the module replicates about thirty
lines of read logic from [[nytid.cli.todo]] and [[nytid.cli.track]]
(\cref{cli.todo,cli.track}).
Duplication drifts, so each replicated behaviour is pinned to the
real implementation by a \emph{drift-guard} test: the test writes
state through the real writers and asserts that our independent
readers report exactly what the real readers report.
If either side changes semantics, the guard fails.

The installed script is named [[prompt-nytid]]---\emph{not}
[[nytid-prompt]]---so that typing [[nyt]] followed by tab still
completes uniquely to [[nytid]] in the shell.

\section{Outline}

The module tangles to the single file [[prompt.py]].
Besides the standard library we import only [[platformdirs]], which
we need to locate the configuration file the same way
[[typerconf]] does (\cref{prompt:config}).

<<[[prompt.py]]>>=
"""Machine-readable nytid status for shell prompts.

Prints two lines: the space-separated ids of the active
(in-progress) todos, and the space-separated labels currently
tracked by `nytid track`.  Designed to be cheap enough to run on
every shell prompt render; it deliberately avoids importing
`nytid.cli`, which would pull in the whole CLI dependency tree.

Installed as the script `prompt-nytid`.
"""

