\section{The command line}

The entry point prints, by default, two lines: the active todo ids
and the active labels, each space-separated, an empty line when
there is nothing.
Two lines with a fixed meaning make the shell side trivial:
\begin{minted}{bash}
readarray -t _nytid < <(prompt-nytid --who "$USER")
# ${_nytid[0]} = active todo ids
# ${_nytid[1]} = tracked labels
\end{minted}

We parse [[sys.argv]] by hand instead of using [[argparse]] or
[[typer]].
[[typer]] is out for the same reason [[nytid.cli]] is: its import
alone costs more than our entire time budget.
[[argparse]] is cheaper but still measurable, and buys nothing
here: there are three options, no values need validation, and a
prompt helper should ignore rather than reject unknown
arguments---[[argparse]]'s error path prints usage and exits~2,
which is exactly the traceback-like behaviour a prompt must never
exhibit.

\begin{description}
\item[The [[--who]] option] selects the worker; the default is the
  [[USER]] environment variable---or [[DEFAULT_WHO]] when it is
  unset---matching what the prompt snippet above passes explicitly.
\item[The [[--todos]] option] prints only the todo line.
\item[The [[--labels]] option] prints only the label line.
\end{description}

<<functions>>=
def main():
    """Print machine-readable status lines for a prompt.

    Line 1: space-separated active todo ids.
    Line 2: space-separated tracked labels for `--who WHO`
    (default: the `USER` environment variable, or `me`).
    `--todos` or `--labels` restricts output to that one line.
    Never raises; on any error it prints the expected number of
    empty lines and returns, so the caller always gets
    well-formed output and exit code 0.
    """
    try:
        <<parse the command line>>
        <<print the requested lines>>
    except Exception:
        <<print empty lines as fallback>>
@

<<parse the command line>>=
args = sys.argv[1:]
who = os.environ.get("USER", DEFAULT_WHO)
only = None
i = 0
while i < len(args):
    if args[i] == "--who" and i + 1 < len(args):
        who = args[i + 1]
        i += 1
    elif args[i] == "--todos":
        only = "todos"
    elif args[i] == "--labels":
        only = "labels"
    i += 1
@

<<print the requested lines>>=
if only != "labels":
    print(" ".join(
        str(todo_id) for todo_id in active_todo_ids()
    ))
if only != "todos":
    print(" ".join(active_labels(who)))
@

The fallback must print as many lines as the caller expects, so it
re-derives the line count from [[sys.argv]] alone---the parsed
state may not exist if the failure happened during parsing.

<<print empty lines as fallback>>=
restricted = (
    "--todos" in sys.argv[1:] or "--labels" in sys.argv[1:]
)
for _ in range(1 if restricted else 2):
    print()
